为了制作《老爸老妈浪漫史》第二季，我们与最棒的《办公室》女星珍娜携手合作。

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:10.019
The best.
最棒的。

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:18.019
And Angela over at OLN, the office ladies network and they loved our show and they said, come on over.
还有OLN（办公室女星网络）的安吉拉，她们很喜欢我们的剧，于是邀请我们加入。

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:29.019
Come under the umbrella that we have so lovingly created and so we just huddled up next to them and we've been working with them for a while now.
来到她们精心打造的大家庭中，我们便紧密团结在一起，已经合作了一段时间了。

00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:30.940
and they're just unbelievable.
她们真是不可思议。

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.340
Jenna Fisher and Angela Kinsey and all the folks over there.
珍娜·费舍尔、安吉拉·金赛以及那里的所有人。

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:40.740
So every once in a while, we'll drop into their podcast and they'll come on to ours and we've done that before.
所以偶尔我们会去她们的播客串门，她们也会来我们的节目，我们之前就做过。

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.939
And we just did a really fun one.
而且我们刚刚完成了一期非常有趣的节目。

00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:45.420
Craig, tell us, tell the audience what we did.
克雷格，告诉观众我们做了什么。

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.060
Yeah, I love they had this really focused idea of let's all talk about our favorite cold opens.
是的，我很喜欢她们提出的一个明确想法，那就是聊聊各自最喜欢的冷开场。

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.899
Cold opens are in a TV show.
电视剧中的冷开场。

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:59.000
Are the funny little mini vignette, the mini little story that kind of opens and kind of smash cuts into the main titles.
是指那些简短有趣的片段，即在片头曲出现前的那段迷你小故事。

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.619
launches the episode.
为剧集拉开序幕。

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:10.200
And so the two of them chose their all-time two favorite office cold opens, and we chose our two favorite, how I met your mother cold opens, and we had this great discussion about them.
于是她们选了自己心中《办公室》最棒的两个冷开场，我们也选了《老爸老妈浪漫史》最棒的两个，并进行了深入讨论。

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:24.599
We play all the clips from them, we talk about them, we dissect them, and we talked about how the cold opens of those two shows were often quite different, and what was brilliant about them, and it was an awesome chat, so we're really excited to share it here.
我们播放了所有片段，讨论并剖析了它们，还聊到这两部剧的冷开场风格迥异之处以及它们的精彩之处，那是一次很棒的交谈，我们很高兴能在这里分享。

00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:31.200
So enjoy our chat with Jenna and Angela about cold opens on the office, and how I met your mother.
那么，请享受我们与珍娜和安吉拉关于《办公室》和《老爸老妈浪漫史》冷开场的聊天吧。

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.560
This is a cold open about cold open.
这是一个关于冷开场的冷开场。

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.840
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the hyper-fixed podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，《Hyper-Fixed》播客的主持人。

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.319
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并尝试帮他们解决。

00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:46.480
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如，“我30岁了，不敢在纽约开车”。

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.239
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，“为什么我倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？”

00:01:50.239 --> 00:01:52.760
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决非汽车相关的问题。

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:58.909
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有困难，我们不仅会解决它，还会揭示导致这些问题的背后隐形系统。

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:01.390
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:13.550
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
在各大播客平台或HyperFixedPod.com即可找到。

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.349
Oh, they're fine, gentlemen.
哦，他们是“绅士”，先生们。

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:16.189
Why hello?
你好？

00:02:16.189 --> 00:02:17.030
Oh, is that us?
哦，是在叫我们吗？

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:17.870
We're the fine.
我们是“绅士”。

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:18.710
That's us for the fine gentlemen.
没错，我们就是“绅士”。

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:19.550
I was so confused.
我刚才太困惑了。

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.030
You are the fine gentlemen.
你们就是绅士。

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.590
My goodness, of course.
天哪，当然了。

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.030
It's so great to be chatting with you again.
能再次与你们交谈真是太棒了。

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.229
We love chatting with you guys.
我们也喜欢和你们聊天。

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:27.389
Thanks for having us back.
谢谢让我们回来。

00:02:27.389 --> 00:02:28.509
Yes, fine gentlemen.
没错，绅士们。

00:02:28.509 --> 00:02:29.590
It's lovely to see you.
很高兴见到你们。

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.030
Top of the morning to you.
早上好。

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:44.550
Well, we're really excited to chat cold opens with you And Craig, it's really cool to have you on the podcast today because you're gonna give us a little bit of, I hope, a writer, showrunners perspective on the cold open.
我们非常兴奋能和你们聊冷开场，克雷格，今天你能上我们的播客真是太棒了，因为我希望能从编剧和制片人的角度听听关于冷开场的见解。

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.870
Could you tell our audience what is a cold open?
你能告诉我们的观众什么是冷开场吗？

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.189
No pressure, by the way, no pressure.
别有压力，随便说说。

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:52.990
Yeah, I know.
是的，我知道。

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.669
I have to define the entire genre of cold open.
我得给整个冷开场流派下定义。

00:02:55.669 --> 00:02:57.870
And this will be canon, whatever you say.
无论我说什么都会成为经典。

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.789
So this will go right in the dictionary.
这都要写进字典里了。

00:03:00.789 --> 00:03:02.789
It's a little funny thing at the start of the show.
就是剧集开头的一点小乐子。

00:03:02.789 --> 00:03:04.430
That's the really short version.
简单版本就是这样。

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:13.909
I think the office was amazing at doing ones that were these little self-contained movies, these little mini short films that kind of that stood alone sometimes.
我觉得《办公室》在制作那些像独立微电影一样的冷开场方面非常出色，它们有时完全可以独立存在。

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:15.229
They didn't always kick off the story.
它们并不总是为了开启正文故事。

00:03:15.229 --> 00:03:16.069
Am I right you guys?
我说得对吗？

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:18.430
It feels like that was often true for you guys.
感觉这对你们来说很常见。

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.069
That's so hard to do that we almost never did it on how I met your mother.
这太难做了，所以我们在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中几乎没这么做过。

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:22.909
We were too lazy.
我们太懒了。

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:25.750
We're like, it's too hard to make a little like gem.
我们觉得要做出那样一个“小宝石”太难了。

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:33.349
So we often, we usually started the story off, somehow in our cold opens, you can either kick off the plot and the cold open, or it's just a funny little thing.
所以我们通常会在冷开场中就开始推进故事。冷开场要么是剧情的切入点，要么纯粹是搞笑片段。

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:35.150
And those are sort of the two ways to go.
这是两种主要的处理方式。

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:41.349
We sometimes did the funny little thing version on how much mother, but usually we try to get the story started in some way.
我们有时也会做纯搞笑版，但通常还是会尝试以某种方式开启故事。

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:47.389
I would argue that doing the funny standalone way is probably harder because when you're starting the story, it at least has some momentum.
我得说，做独立搞笑版可能更难，因为如果是开启故事，至少自带动力。

00:03:47.389 --> 00:03:50.750
But you guys did it ingeniously on the office.
但你们在《办公室》里处理得非常巧妙。

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:55.110
Well, you know, we all today picked one of our favorite cold opens from each of our shows.
好吧，今天我们要各自选一个最喜欢的冷开场。

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:58.270
We're gonna share those, we'll set them up and we'll talk about them.
我们要分享这些片段，进行背景介绍并讨论。

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:08.469
And I know the one I picked, I'm not gonna say it yet, but I almost picked the episode, The Injury, and that was a time we had a cold open on the office that did kick off the episode.
我知道我选的那个，虽然我还没说，但我差点选了《受伤》那一集，那是我们在《办公室》中难得的一个不仅是冷开场，还开启了整集故事的例子。

00:04:08.469 --> 00:04:15.069
That's when Michael has a George Foreman grill by his bed because he loves the fresh bacon in the morning and he steps on the grill.
那是迈克尔在床边放了一个乔治·福尔曼烤架，因为他喜欢早上吃新鲜培根，结果脚踩在了烤架上。

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:21.389
But so that cold open where he calls Pam in a panic is one that then leads into the whole episode.
那个他惊慌失措地给帕姆打电话的冷开场，直接引出了整集故事。

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:23.509
So we did that every once in a while, but you're right.
所以我们偶尔也会这样做，但你是对的。

00:04:23.509 --> 00:04:26.310
Mostly ours were these standalone little movies.
大多数情况下，我们的冷开场都是独立的小短片。

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:35.149
Did you guys tell us in one of the episodes we did together that often they would write it for one and it would end up on another episode, like they would mix and match them.
你们在之前的一集里告诉过我们，有时候编剧写好的内容会被挪用到另一集，就像混搭一样。

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:38.509
They were always the ones that you thought you were, they were gonna be paired with.
它们总是最初配对的那种吗？

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:39.709
Yes, exactly.
是的，完全正确。

00:04:39.709 --> 00:04:52.550
Because they were movable, because they were their own little story, but also sometimes they just got rid of them completely because they needed the time to tell the story of the episode.
因为它们是可移动的，属于独立小故事。但也有些会被完全删掉，因为需要时间来讲述当集的主要剧情。

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:58.870
Right, and if it is self-contained and modular like that, it's easier to take out, whereas if you start the story, you're kind of stuck with it.
没错，如果它是独立且模块化的，就更容易删减；而如果它开启了故事，你就必须保留它。

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.149
But you guys always do a cold open because we didn't do it every episode.
但你们每一集都有冷开场吗？因为我们不是每一集都有。

00:05:02.149 --> 00:05:04.870
We did sort of longer like full act ones.
我们通常有比较长的完整第一幕。

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:14.149
We started the show with titles for the entire first season and then kind of mixed and matched although we realized like in season two that we had a great theme song to smash cut to.
第一季我们全程以片头曲开场，后来开始混搭，不过在第二季我们意识到我们有一个很棒的主题曲可以用来强行转场。

00:05:14.149 --> 00:05:15.149
Yeah.
是的。

00:05:15.149 --> 00:05:16.149
Do you know what I mean?
你懂我的意思吧？

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:20.949
With that like hard cut to the ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-ba, it's like, it's perfect for that.
那种硬切到主题曲的感觉，非常完美。

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:21.949
How are we not?
我们当时在想，为什么我们没这么做？

00:05:21.949 --> 00:05:22.949
We were like, why are we doing this?
其实我想说，我们两部剧都有那种标志性的、让人印象深刻的主题曲。

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:28.220
I was going to say though, both our shows have really iconic grab you by the lapels kind of theme songs.
而且如果你在第一季就建立了主题曲的认知，到了第二季，再用冷开场转接到主题曲会非常刺激。

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:33.339
And there's something especially about if you do the first season, people get to know the theme song.
是的，我觉得我们的主题曲有时就像场景结束时的那个“句号”，对吧？

00:05:33.339 --> 00:05:40.029
By the second season, it's actually really thrilling to do a cold open and then cut, you know, do the needle drop to the theme song.
是的。

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:45.545
Yeah, I feel like our theme songs were almost the button at the end of the scene sometimes, right?
克雷格，你知道，我们偶尔还是会有冷开场的。

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:46.045
Yeah.
有时为了时长会省略，但大部分情况下都有。

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:50.041
You know, Craig, we did usually have a cold open every once in a while.
我想到我们的编剧室，他们想出这些独立冷开场点子时有多开心。

00:05:50.041 --> 00:05:52.968
We wouldn't for time, but for the most part, we did.
比如凯文的辣椒，天哪。

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:59.328
And I think about our writers room and and how much fun they had coming up with these standalone cold open ideas.
那是亚伦·舒尔写的。

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.670
I mean, Kevin's chili, my lord.
我想布莱恩这辈子都会被人引用这个梗。

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.709
That one, that was Aaron Schur.
而且我觉得，如果墙上有个卡片，写着一个好笑的点子，但做不了一整集，把它做成两分钟的搞笑片段就很棒。

00:06:03.709 --> 00:06:09.269
And I just, people quote that to Brian, I think for the rest of his life, he's gonna hear that.
我认为我们很多冷开场对于那种伪纪录片风格来说可能太荒诞了，但我们在冷开场里可以放得更开。说到更广阔的冷开场，安吉拉，说说你的选择。

00:06:09.269 --> 00:06:13.709
I think also, it's a great way if you have a card on the wall.
那是我们的经典之一。

00:06:13.709 --> 00:06:21.110
That's a funny idea, but you just can't make it a whole episode, but you could make it a really funny two minutes.
我选了一个全员参与的冷开场，因为有时候冷开场大多是迈克尔或德怀特的个人线。但这个场景有所有人，还包括了摄像师和吊杆麦克风操作员的大规模排练。

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:41.209
I think a lot of our cold opens were things also that might be feeling a little too absurd for our little documentary show that we were putting on, but we could be a little more broad in our cold opens as well, which brings us to speaking of a little bit of a broader cold open, Angela, your pick.
简直就像是一场真正的舞蹈。

00:06:41.209 --> 00:06:43.269
It's one of our classics.
我在机场还会被人冲着喊这个梗。

00:06:43.269 --> 00:06:58.509
So I wanted to pick a cold open that was a real all ensemble moment because sometimes the cold opens were mostly, you know, Michael or Dwight and those kinds of storylines, But this had everybody, it included a huge rehearsal with our camera operators, our boom operators.
这来自《减压》那一集，就是那个著名的火灾演习冷开场，德怀特要教大家如何在办公室火灾中生存。

00:06:58.509 --> 00:07:01.550
It was like a real dance, if you will.
方法是放火。

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:04.829
It's something I get yelled at the airport, okay?
锁上门，然后放火。

00:07:04.829 --> 00:07:17.750
So basically this is from Stress Relief, the episode Stress Relief, it is that famous fire drill cold open where Dwight is gonna teach everyone how to survive an office place fire and all the procedures.
瞬间陷入绝对的混乱。

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.149
By setting a fire.
我们截取了中间的一段片段，当时德怀特在混乱中试图让大家遵守步骤。

00:07:19.149 --> 00:07:21.589
By locking the doors and setting a fire.
来看一下。

00:07:21.589 --> 00:07:23.829
It's absolute instant mayhem.
见过枪击受害者吗？

00:07:23.829 --> 00:07:29.790
We pulled a clip from the middle where Dwight is trying to get everyone to do all the steps in the chaos.
程序，程序，出口选择。

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.069
Here it is.
我们要去哪，伙计们？

00:07:31.069 --> 00:07:34.120
You ever seen a gun victim?
用湿巾捂住。

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.879
Procedure, procedure, exit option.
什么？

00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:39.279
Where do we go, folks?
湿抹布，也许吧。

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:40.800
Use a watch to cover them out.
记住那些程序。

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:41.879
What?
动作是什么？

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:44.879
A rag, a damp rag, perhaps.
那是错的路，我们试过了。

00:07:44.879 --> 00:07:46.439
Let's remember those procedures.
记住出口点，大家，出口点。

00:07:46.439 --> 00:07:47.360
What are the actions?
下一步呢？

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.720
That's the wrong way, we've already tried that.
活下去，我要回家。

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.519
Remember your exit points, exit points, people.
你太沉了。

00:07:53.519 --> 00:07:54.600
What's next?
打911。

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.160
Stay alive, I'm getting home.
天哪，太棒了。

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:06.540
You're too heavy.
太精彩了。

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:11.600
You won, nine, one, one.
能问个问题吗？

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.000
Oh my God, it's so good.
拍这个花了多久？看起来像整部电影一样。

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.519
It's so great.
拍了一整天，但排练也花了不少时间。

00:08:16.519 --> 00:08:17.360
Can I ask a question?
是的，我提前一天到了，要和那只猫还有训猫师配合，我们准备了假猫和真猫。我还用了替身，那个把猫扔向天花板并从另一边掉出来的特技是个大工程。

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.600
How long did that take to shoot that?
我特别喜欢那种出其不意，第一次扔猫很搞笑，但没过多久那只猫又掉下来，那才是最让人爆笑的时刻。

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.240
That looks like an entire movie.
那只猫完全没事。

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.759
Well, it was a full day to shoot it, but it was also a bunch of rehearsal.
听众朋友们，我们的制片人放了一堆软垫。

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:43.960
Yeah, I came in a day early and I had to work with this cat and the cat wrangler and we had a fake cat, we had a real cat and a fake cat, and I had a body double, like that stunt, just that moment of throwing the cat in the ceiling and it coming out the other side was a huge stunt.
所以它掉在软软的东西上了。

00:08:43.960 --> 00:09:00.120
I just love like the unexpected, like the first throw of the cat is hilarious and unexpected, but so soon after is that second falling cat, which you're not quite expecting, which is like the actual enormous laugh of that moment.
